# requirements.in — DECLARED dependencies for the analysis core (F24 + F25).
#
# This is the package.json of the repo: hand-edited, loosely constrained, lists
# only DIRECT imports. The package-lock.json equivalent is requirements.txt,
# which is generated from this file and pins every transitive dependency.
#
#   pip install pip-tools
#   pip-compile requirements.in          -o requirements.txt
#   pip-compile requirements-extract.in  -o requirements-extract.txt
#   pip-compile requirements-wavlm.in    -o requirements-wavlm.txt
#
# Install the locked set (the `npm ci` equivalent):
#
#   pip install -r requirements.txt
#
# THE TIERS ARE ALTERNATIVES, NOT ADDITIVE. Each .txt is an independent
# resolution, and they disagree: numba (via librosa) caps numpy at 2.4.6, while
# the core tier resolves to 2.5.1. Installing the core then the wavlm tier
# silently DOWNGRADES numpy, leaving an environment that matches neither lock.
# For one environment that runs everything (e.g. the `gayvoice` conda env), use
# the combined lock instead -- a single consistent resolution of all three:
#
#   pip-compile requirements.in requirements-extract.in requirements-wavlm.in \
#       -o requirements-all.txt
#   pip install -r requirements-all.txt
#
# Interpreter is pinned in .python-version (3.13) — the `.nvmrc` equivalent.
# Bounds below are floors, not the versions the published results were produced
# with: no manifest existed when those ran, and that interpreter is gone from
# the original machine, so the historical environment is not recoverable. The
# lockfiles guarantee reproducibility from here forward, not retroactively.
#
# NOT covered here (not pip-installable — install separately):
#   - Montreal Forced Aligner (conda: `conda install -c conda-forge montreal-forced-aligner`)
#   - Praat / FastTrack (F24 formant tracking, standalone app)

# --- shared numeric + plotting core ---------------------------------------
numpy>=1.26
